-- Runner — assertions about a course nobody drew.
--
-- The interesting property is not that the bird flies; it is that the *same*
-- course exists on every console. `stream` composed it from the seed at compile
-- time, so a Game Boy and a Mega Drive get identical tilemaps and differ only in
-- how much of one they can show. A test that passes on one and fails on another
-- would mean the composition had picked up a per-console dependency, which is
-- exactly the bug this file is here to catch.

test the bird drifts down and to the right on its own
play 20 ticks
expect bird1.ydirection > 0
expect bird1.x > 1

-- Ten ticks, not thirty: the corridor is four rows and the bird falls out of the
-- bottom of it in about half a second, which is the difference between this and
-- the drifting version it replaced.
test flapping lifts it
play 10 ticks
press a
play 4 ticks
expect bird1.ydirection < 0

-- The one assertion here written in absolute cells, and deliberately so: 24
-- chunks of 6 is 144 cells on *every* console, because the seed and the chunks
-- decide the course and the console decides nothing. If composition ever picked
-- up a per-console dependency, this is the line that would fail.
test the composed course is the same size on every console
play 2 ticks
expect levelwidth = 144
expect levelheight = 30
expect levelwidth > screenwidth
expect levelheight > screenheight

test an unflown bird ends up in the scenery
play 600 ticks
expect scene gameover

test the coin is always somewhere in the level
play 40 ticks
expect prize1.x >= 0
expect prize1.right <= levelwidth
expect prize1.y >= 0
expect prize1.bottom <= levelheight
